\details{
\subsection{The Algorithm}{

This implementation of OPTICS implements the original
algorithm as described by Ankerst et al (1999). OPTICS is an ordering
algorithm with methods to extract a clustering from the ordering.
While using similar concepts as DBSCAN, \code{minPts} in OPTICS has a different
effect than in DBSCAN. Since it is also used to calculate the reachability
distance, larger values will make the reachability distance plot smoother.
The parameter \code{eps} is optional. It represents
an upper limit for the neighborhood size used to reduce
computational complexity which is helpful for large data sets.
By default (\code{eps = NULL}), it is estimated from \code{minPts} and the data. If the
data contain well-separated groups, the estimate can result in several
undefined (\code{Inf}) reachability distances. Use \code{eps = Inf} to
not limit the neighborhood size.

OPTICS linearly orders the data points such that points which are spatially
closest become neighbors in the ordering. The closest analog to this
ordering is dendrogram in single-link hierarchical clustering. The algorithm
also calculates the reachability distance for each point.
\code{plot()} (see \link{reachability_plot})
produces a reachability plot which shows each points reachability distance
between two consecutive points
where the points are sorted by OPTICS. Valleys represent clusters (the
deeper the valley, the more dense the cluster) and high points indicate
points between clusters.
}

\subsection{Specifying the Data}{

If \code{x} is specified as a data matrix, then Euclidean distances and fast
nearest neighbor lookup using a kd-tree are used. See \code{\link[=kNN]{kNN()}} for
details on the parameters for the kd-tree.
}

\subsection{The effect of eps}{

The \code{eps} parameter can be used to restrict the considered maximum epsilon
neighborhood size which improves performance significantly. Setting
\code{eps = Inf} does not restrict the neighborhood and results in the complete
reachability structure (only the first reachability distance is undefined).
However, this is can be slower for large data sets.

To improve speed, the default value of \code{NULL}
calculates a reasonable eps as the largest distance to a (\code{minPts - 1})-th
nearest neighbor from \code{minPts} and the data. This gives every point
a valid core distance, but if the data contain very well-separated groups,
then this radius can result in
several undefined reachability distances (shown as dashed lines in the
reachability plot). If the plot shows too many dashed lines then
\code{eps} should be increased.
}

\subsection{Ties}{

OPTICS repeatedly processes the unprocessed point with the smallest
reachability distance. If several points have the same reachability distance,
ties are broken by the position of the point in the data: the point with the
lower id (row number) is processed first. This follows the ELKI
implementation. With ties, the returned order (and therefore the
reachability plot and extracted clusters) can depend on the row order of
the data. Ties are common for data with repeated values or for
data on a regular grid.
}

\subsection{Extracting a Clustering}{

Several methods to extract a clustering from the order returned by OPTICS are
implemented:
\itemize{
\item \code{extractDBSCAN()} extracts a clustering from an OPTICS ordering that is
similar to what DBSCAN would produce with an eps set to \code{eps_cl} (see
Ankerst et al, 1999). The only difference to a DBSCAN clustering is that
OPTICS is not able to assign some border points and reports them instead as
noise.
\item \code{extractXi()} extract clusters hierarchically specified in Ankerst et al
(1999) based on the steepness of the reachability plot. One interpretation
of the \code{xi} parameter is that it classifies clusters by change in
relative cluster density. The algorithm is a port of the implementation in
the ELKI framework (version 0.8.0). It follows Ankerst et al (1999) with the
improvements described in Schubert and Gertz (2018).
With \code{correctPredecessors = TRUE} (the default), the end of
each cluster is shortened so that the predecessor of its last
point is inside the cluster. This step is not part of the original
algorithm and removes a common artifact (found by the ELKI framework).
Clusters with fewer than \code{minPts} points are not reported.
}
}

\subsection{Predict Cluster Memberships}{

\code{predict()} requires an extracted DBSCAN clustering with \code{extractDBSCAN()} and then
uses predict for \code{dbscan()}.
}
}
\examples{
set.seed(2)
n <- 400

x <- cbind(
  x = runif(4, 0, 1) + rnorm(n, sd = 0.1),
  y = runif(4, 0, 1) + rnorm(n, sd = 0.1)
  )

plot(x, col=rep(1:4, times = 100))

### run OPTICS (Note: we use the default eps calculation)
res <- optics(x, minPts = 10)
res

### get order
res$order

### plot produces a reachability plot
plot(res)

### plot the order of points in the reachability plot
plot(x, col = "grey")
polygon(x[res$order, ])

### extract a DBSCAN clustering by cutting the reachability plot at eps_cl
res <- extractDBSCAN(res, eps_cl = .065)
res

plot(res)  ## black is noise
hullplot(x, res)

### re-cut at a higher eps threshold
res <- extractDBSCAN(res, eps_cl = .07)
res
plot(res)
hullplot(x, res)

### extract hierarchical clustering of varying density using the Xi method
res <- extractXi(res, xi = 0.01)
res

plot(res)
hullplot(x, res)

# Xi cluster structure
res$clusters_xi

### use OPTICS on a precomputed distance matrix
d <- dist(x)
res <- optics(d, minPts = 10)
plot(res)
}
